+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>What a management company actually does, and what software covers</h2>
+    <p>The honest version of this list is the whole decision. Software now runs the office half of the job. The physical half (showings, inspections, being reachable) stays with you or a per-task vendor.</p>
+    <div class="tbl-scroll"><table class="cmp">
+      <tr><th>Task</th><th>Can software do it?</th></tr>
+      <tr><td>Advertise and list vacant units (syndication to Zillow, Apartments.com, etc.)</td><td class="">Mostly - software pushes one listing to the major portals automatically; photos and pricing judgment remain human</td></tr><tr><td>Schedule and run showings</td><td class="">Partially - scheduling tools and self-showing lockboxes handle booking; someone local still opens doors or checks the property after</td></tr><tr><td>Tenant screening (credit, criminal, eviction history, income verification)</td><td class="">Mostly - TransUnion SmartMove-class reports run instantly inside software; the accept/reject decision and consistent criteria are the owner&#039;s</td></tr><tr><td>Lease preparation and signing</td><td class="">Mostly - state-specific templates plus e-signature; a local attorney review of the base lease is a one-time cost, not a monthly one</td></tr><tr><td>Move-in / move-out inspections and condition documentation</td><td class="bad">No - requires physical presence; apps only standardize the checklist and photo record</td></tr><tr><td>Rent collection</td><td class="good">Fully - ACH/card autopay, tenant portal, automatic receipts and ledger posting; 78-86% of renters prefer paying online anyway (Buildium/AppFolio)</td></tr><tr><td>Late-payment follow-up, reminders, and late fees</td><td class="good">Fully - automatic reminders before due date, auto-assessed late fees, escalating notices without the owner touching it</td></tr><tr><td>Bookkeeping, owner statements, and tax-time reporting</td><td class="">Mostly - every payment and expense posts to the ledger automatically with Schedule E-ready exports; a CPA still files</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance request intake and dispatch</td><td class="">Mostly - tenants submit requests with photos through a portal, software routes to the assigned vendor and tracks status; the owner still needs vendor relationships and approves big-ticket work</td></tr><tr><td>24/7 emergency response</td><td class="">Partially - intake, triage rules, and vendor routing are automatable; a human plumber still has to show up at 2am</td></tr><tr><td>Vendor management (COIs, invoices, 1099s)</td><td class="">Partially - software tracks documents, invoices, and issues 1099s; finding and keeping good vendors is human work</td></tr><tr><td>Routine property inspections / drive-bys</td><td class="bad">No - physical task; software only schedules and documents it</td></tr><tr><td>Lease renewals and rent increases</td><td class="">Mostly - renewal offers, market-rent comps, and e-sign renewals are automated; the pricing call is the owner&#039;s</td></tr><tr><td>Security deposit handling and itemized deductions</td><td class="">Mostly - escrow tracking, deadlines, and itemization letters are automated; state-specific rules still need to be set correctly once</td></tr><tr><td>Evictions and court appearances</td><td class="bad">No - attorney and court process; software&#039;s contribution is a clean payment ledger and notice trail that wins cases</td></tr><tr><td>Fair housing / legal compliance</td><td class="">Partially - software enforces identical screening criteria and documented processes, which is most of fair-housing defense; knowing local law is not automatable</td></tr>
+    </table></div>
