+</section><section>
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>Where the rent sits each month</h2>
+    <p>With a management company, rent lands in the manager's trust account. You get the remainder after fees on their statement schedule, usually mid-month or later, and repair costs come out before you see them, with any markup already applied. Most owner complaints about managers are some version of this paragraph: slow statements, unclear deductions, surprises at payout time.</p>
+    <p>Self-managing with software inverts that. Rent goes from the tenant's bank to yours through the payment processor, every charge and payment sits in a ledger you can open any hour, and repair invoices are numbers you approved rather than line items you discover. RenPro's books update with every payment, and the built-in AI answers questions about any number on the statement because it reads the same ledger.</p>
+  </div>
+</section><section>
+  <div class="wrap">
+    <h2>When hiring a Myrtle Beach management company still makes sense</h2>
+    <p>This page argues for self-managing with software because the math usually favors it. Not always. The honest exceptions:</p>
+    <ul class="checks"><li>Out-of-state and remote owners: maintenance and emergency response is the single hardest thing to do from hundreds of miles away; a local manager provides around-the-clock physical response and an existing vendor network (Redfin, &#039;Managing a Rental Property Out of State&#039;; Baselane, &#039;How to Manage Out-of-State Rental Property&#039;). For short-term rentals managed from out of state, the guidance is that hiring a manager is almost always advisable despite 25-40% fees.</li><li>Scale: the Census/HUD 2021 Rental Housing Finance Survey shows professional management climbing from ~22% of 1-4 unit properties to 84% of 150+ unit properties - past a certain door count you are hiring management labor either way, whether it&#039;s a PM firm or your own staff.</li><li>Eviction-heavy or heavily regulated markets: an average eviction runs $3,500 and can reach $10,000+ (TransUnion SmartMove; Snappt), and procedural mistakes restart the clock and burn additional months of rent (~$2,540 average lost rent). A PM firm with an attorney pipeline and process discipline earns its fee if evictions are a recurring reality in your market.</li><li>Owners with no time or high-value time: Census-derived data (iPropertyManagement) shows roughly 1 in 6 landlord-managed properties consumes 20-79 hours/month. If your property is a high-touch building or your working hour is worth more than the fee, 8-12% is a rational trade.</li><li>Honest caveat that cuts both ways: UK survey data (Pegasus Insight, 2026) found landlords&#039; reported time commitment was broadly similar whether or not they used an agent - hiring a PM buys execution, not zero involvement; the owner still oversees, approves, and audits.</li></ul>
+  </div>
